TODOCOCCUS - PRIVACY POLICY
Last updated: 25 August 2026


THE SHORT VERSION

Todococcus has no servers. It reads your Reminders on your device and
stores your creatures and quest history in your own iCloud account.
There is no analytics, no advertising, no third-party data sharing, and
no account to create. The developer cannot see your data.


WHO PROVIDES THIS APP

Todococcus is made by an independent developer for use by families.
Questions about this policy, or about anything the app does, can go to
41.hauteur.boiler@icloud.com.


WHAT THE APP READS FROM YOUR DEVICE

Reminders

With your explicit permission, Todococcus reads your Reminders lists in
order to turn reminders into quests. It reads reminder titles, due
dates, completion status, list membership, and any hashtags you have
added for assignment or grouping.

This happens on your device. Reminder content is never transmitted to
the developer or to any third party. If you decline the permission, or
revoke it later in Settings > Privacy & Security > Reminders, the app
has no quests to show you but is otherwise unaffected.


HEALTH DATA

Health features are off unless an adult household member turns them on.
They are available to adult accounts only; children's creatures never
read health data.

When an adult enables them, Todococcus reads selected data from Apple's
Health app for one purpose: to recognise that an activity was logged.
The app rewards the act of logging, not the value logged. Read access
is requested for specific data types only, is granted by you per type,
and can be changed at any time in Settings > Health > Data Access &
Devices.

Health data is read on your device. It is not stored in iCloud, not
transmitted to the developer, and not shared with any third party. It
is never used for advertising, marketing, or data mining. The app
writes nothing back into HealthKit.


WHAT THE APP STORES, AND WHERE

Todococcus creates and stores:

  - Creature state: species, evolution stage, experience, and earned
    visual features
  - Quest completion records, including which household member
    completed which quest and when
  - Local app settings and preferences

This information lives in two places: on your device, and in Apple's
CloudKit inside your own iCloud account. Household members share a
single CloudKit record zone so that everyone sees the same creatures
and the same quest history.

Records in a CloudKit private or shared database are not accessible to
app developers. The developer of Todococcus cannot read, export,
modify, or delete anything in your household's zone.

Health data is deliberately excluded from everything above. Nothing
read from the Health app, and nothing from which a health value could
be reconstructed, is written to CloudKit.


WHAT THE APP DOES NOT DO

  - No advertising, and no advertising identifiers
  - No analytics or crash-reporting SDKs of any kind
  - No third-party servers; there is no backend other than Apple's
    CloudKit
  - No account creation, email address, password, or sign-in of its own
  - No location data
  - No sale, rental, or sharing of data with anyone
  - No tracking across apps or websites, so no App Tracking
    Transparency prompt


BETA TESTING THROUGH TESTFLIGHT

While Todococcus is distributed through TestFlight, Apple - not the app
- collects crash logs and basic usage metrics such as session counts,
installs, and crashes, and makes them available to the developer.
Testers can also submit screenshots and written feedback through
TestFlight; that feedback goes to the developer and will contain
whatever was on screen when it was captured.

This collection is governed by Apple's Privacy Policy
(https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/) and the TestFlight Terms of
Service
(https://www.apple.com/legal/internet-services/itunes/testflight/). It
stops when the app leaves TestFlight.


HOUSEHOLDS AND CHILDREN

Todococcus is designed to be used by a family together. Within a
household, members can see each other's creatures and completed quests
- that shared visibility is the point of the app. It is confined to the
household; nothing crosses out of it.

The family organizer sets up the household and controls who is invited
to the shared zone. No information about any household member, adult or
child, is transmitted to the developer or to any third party.

Apple's TestFlight Terms of Service do not permit children under 13 to
use TestFlight, so children in that age range are not beta testers.

A parent or guardian who wants a child's data removed can delete it
from within the app or remove the household's iCloud data directly. The
developer holds no separate copy and therefore has nothing to delete on
request.


DELETING YOUR DATA

Deleting the app removes local data. Data held in CloudKit can be
removed from within the app, or through Settings > [your name] >
iCloud > Manage Account Storage. Because the data lives in your iCloud
account rather than on a developer-operated server, you control it
directly and no request to the developer is needed.


CHANGES TO THIS POLICY

Any change will be reflected on this page along with a revised date at
the top. Material changes - particularly any change to what the app
reads or stores - will also be called out in release notes.


